metadata.rules_text:
{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last ~5–10 days show an upswing, add a positive level shift to the whole path (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR too low, growing with horizon).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small, but from ~day 7 onward the model increasingly undershoots the true path; apply an upward ramp correction that increases with lead time.",
    "Trend/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to bullish breaks (it stays anchored to recent lows), so increase slope when recent momentum is positive (e.g., if 5d return > 0, steepen forecast trajectory).",
    "Spike handling: after upside spikes the model often mean-reverts too aggressively and misses sustained strength; reduce mean-reversion strength following a breakout and keep elevated levels for longer (carry-through premium).",
    "Compression of volatility: forecasts are too smooth vs outcomes; widen forecast range by increasing day-to-day variability, especially during trending periods (avoid flattening around ~68–70 when market is pushing >71–73)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model systematically underpredicts during rising regimes; apply an upward level shift (≈ +1 to +3 €/t on average), increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to upswings; add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope (if recent slope > 0, boost the whole path, especially days 7–25).",
    "Horizon-specific underestimation: mid-to-long horizon (≈ day 10–25) shows the largest miss as true prices grind higher; apply a ramping correction that grows with lead time.",
    "Spike handling: after sharp upside moves, the model often mean-reverts too quickly and caps the peak; allow higher peak persistence (reduce mean-reversion strength for 1–2 weeks after an upside break).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk: when the model finally turns bullish it can overshoot at the far end (e.g., projecting continued surge while realized prices roll over); cap late-horizon drift using a volatility/mean-reversion constraint when recent realized momentum weakens."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing (higher highs / positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path; the underprediction is largest at mid-horizons (~day 10–22).",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to breakouts; accelerate the trend component (steepen the path) during the first ~2 weeks after a regime change upward.",
    "Peak underestimation: when history contains a sharp jump (spike/breakout), the model caps subsequent highs; increase projected local maxima by ~1–3 EUR in the following 1–3 weeks.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: beyond ~day 25–30 the model often stays too high versus realized pullbacks; apply stronger mean reversion / downward adjustment at the tail when the forecast implies continued elevation after an extended rally.",
    "Horizon-shaped correction: apply a hump-shaped bias correction (small at days 1–5, largest positive at days ~10–22, then taper to neutral/negative by days ~25–30)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rise, the model systematically under-forecasts levels for ~days 3–20 (often by ~+1 to +3 €/t). Apply an additive uplift that increases with horizon (small at day 1–2, larger mid-horizon).",
    "Spike/impulse lag: after sharp upward moves, the model responds too slowly (forecasts stay near the pre-spike regime). Use a momentum carry rule: if a 1–3 day change > ~+2 €/t, shift the next ~5–10 forecast days upward by ~40–70% of that move.",
    "Peak underestimation: local maxima are consistently too low (model smooths peaks). If recent volatility is high (e.g., range > ~3 €/t over 10 days), widen forecast amplitude by scaling deviations from the forecast mean upward (i.e., amplify highs).",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: beyond ~day 20–30 the model tends to predict too-steep declines/mean reversion (e.g., forecasting drops that occur later or not as strongly). Damp negative slopes in the far horizon (shrink forecast day-to-day decreases by ~30–50%).",
    "Turning-point timing error: the model often calls the downturn too early after rallies. Add a persistence heuristic: if price is above its 10–20d average and still rising, delay any predicted reversal by ~3–7 days (keep levels elevated longer)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward level shift to the whole path (roughly +1 to +3 EUR early horizon), because forecasts systematically underpredict rallies.",
    "Underestimation of upside volatility: after a breakout/spike day, increase the next 3–10 day forecasts more aggressively (the model smooths/attenuates moves and misses follow-through).",
    "Horizon-dependent sign flip: short horizon (≈days 1–15) tends to be too low during rallies, while longer horizon (≈days 20–30) tends to be too high (model mean-reverts too slowly and keeps levels elevated). Apply a tapering correction: positive adjustment early that decays and can turn slightly negative late.",
    "Lag around turning points: the model reacts late to regime changes; if recent realized returns change sign (trend reversal), pull the forecast toward the new direction faster (increase mean-reversion speed).",
    "Over-persistence after peaks: when spot has recently peaked and starts falling, reduce days 15–30 forecasts (model keeps prices too high and misses the subsequent drawdown)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising markets: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing, the model underpredicts the next ~1–15 days by roughly +1 to +3 €/t; apply an upward level shift early in the horizon.",
    "Peak/impulse underestimation: sharp upside jumps (e.g., breakout days) are systematically missed; add an extra +1 to +2 €/t on top of the trend correction for the first ~3–8 forecast days after an upside breakout.",
    "Horizon-dependent sign flip: the model tends to stay too high at longer horizons when the true path mean-reverts/later sells off; from ~day 20 onward, fade the forecast toward the recent mean (or apply a mild negative drift) rather than extrapolating elevated levels.",
    "Over-smoothness / lag: forecasts react too slowly to regime changes (both rallies and subsequent reversals); increase responsiveness by anchoring more weight to the most recent 3–7 observations versus the 30-day history.",
    "Late-horizon drawdown miss: when the recent history contains a spike to new highs, the model often fails to price the subsequent drop; impose stronger mean-reversion penalties after a spike, especially beyond ~2–3 weeks."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime shift / trend bias: the model persistently underpredicts during the early uptrend phase (roughly first 1–2 weeks ahead) and then overpredicts during the subsequent downtrend phase (later horizons). Add a trend-regime detector; apply +1 to +3 €/t uplift when recent momentum is strongly positive, and a -2 to -6 €/t haircut when momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: forecast paths are too flat/anchored near ~69–71, while realized prices show larger directional moves. Increase slope magnitude with horizon (i.e., amplify the recent trend for days 1–10), but enforce stronger mean reversion for days 15–30.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after a sharp realized rise, the model extrapolates higher levels (often into low/mid-70s) while the true series mean-reverts and can sell off hard. Cap post-spike upside: if last 5–10 days include a >3–4 €/t jump, dampen all horizons by ~30–50% of the model’s projected additional gains and pull the level back toward a 10–20d moving average.",
    "Missed reversal timing (lag): turning points occur earlier in reality than in forecasts (model keeps the prior trend for too long). Introduce an earlier reversal trigger: if price fails to make new highs for 2–3 sessions after a rally (or breaks below a short MA), start declining the path within 3–5 days rather than 10–15.",
    "Tail risk underestimation on the downside (late horizon): the model rarely projects deep drops, but outcomes can fall ~5–10 €/t below forecasts by days 20–30. Add a downside skew adjustment: when volatility is elevated or momentum turns negative, subtract an additional -2 €/t (days 15–20) widening to -4 to -8 €/t (days 21–30)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime bias: forecasts are too low during short bullish runs (days 1–15 in ex.1–3), then become too high when the market turns bearish (ex.4–5). Apply a sign-dependent bias: add ~+1 to +2 EUR in rising regimes; subtract ~-2 to -5 EUR in falling regimes.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts late to reversals. When the last 3–5 realized closes show a clear direction change, pull the whole path toward the new trend (increase slope in upturns; decrease slope in downturns) rather than keeping the prior drift.",
    "Overshoot / insufficient drawdown after peaks: after a local high, the model keeps levels elevated and does not capture the subsequent sell-off magnitude (ex.4–5: forecasts stay ~70–74 while outcomes fall to mid-60s/low-60s). Impose stronger mean reversion after peaks: cap upside persistence and accelerate declines over horizons 10–30d.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (1–7d) errors are modest; mid/long horizon (10–30d) errors widen materially, especially in down moves. Apply an increasing correction with horizon (e.g., scale the bias/mean-reversion adjustment up with lead time).",
    "Volatility underestimation in transitions: the model smooths too much around regime shifts (misses both the early rally strength and later crash depth). Increase volatility/dispersion conditional on large recent moves (e.g., if |5d return| is high, widen adjustments and steepen trend)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Missed regime shift / slow to price drawdowns: when the recent history contains a local peak (≈70–72) followed by weakening, the model keeps prices elevated instead of following the downtrend. Add a trend-following correction that pulls the path down when the last 5–10 days slope is negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after upward spikes in history, the model projects continued high/flat levels; true outcomes often mean-revert and then sell off. Introduce mean-reversion: cap upside continuation and fade forecasts back toward a rolling mean (e.g., 10–20d average) after large positive moves.",
    "Horizon-shape error: the model’s forward curve is too flat (insufficient decline) during downturns; realized path shows a pronounced drop in weeks 3–4. Add a convexity adjustment: progressively lower days 15–30 when short-term momentum is negative.",
    "Underestimation of volatility in the downside tail: realized outcomes exhibit sharper late-horizon drops (mid-60s) than forecast. Widen downside risk by applying an extra negative adjustment when recent volatility is elevated (e.g., large 5–10d range)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift / level bias: when the last 5–10 days show a clear downside break (new local lows), the model stays anchored to the prior ~70–72 level. Apply a downward level adjustment that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are mildly low in the first ~1–7 days during the late-July/early-Aug upswing, but become strongly high beyond ~day 15–30 as the realized path mean-reverts lower. Use a piecewise bias correction: near-term +0.3–0.8, mid/long-term −3 to −10 depending on recent trend strength.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after a run-up to ~71–73, the model projects persistence/continuation, while outcomes peak then fall sharply. Add a spike-detection rule (e.g., >+3 over 5–7 days) that forces mean reversion in the forecast tail (days 10–30) rather than trend continuation.",
    "Lag in turning points: the model reacts ~5–10 days late to the downturn; it keeps predicting flat-to-up while the market is already declining. Incorporate a faster trend filter (higher weight on last 3–7 observations) to flip the sign of the drift earlier.",
    "Underestimation of drawdown magnitude: when declines start, the model’s slope is too shallow and volatility too low, missing the drop into low/mid-60s. Increase downside convexity/vol scaling in bearish regimes (e.g., amplify negative drift by ~1.5–2x beyond day 10 when short-term momentum is negative)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-change blind spot: when the last ~5–10 days show a rollover/incipient downtrend after a local peak, the model keeps projecting elevated/sideways levels. Apply a downward level-shift to the whole path once momentum turns negative (e.g., if 5d return < 0 and price is below its 10d mean).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly low in the first ~7–12 days (miss upside continuation), but become strongly high beyond ~day 15–30 (miss drawdowns). Use a piecewise correction: small +adjustment early, increasing -adjustment with horizon after day ~12–15.",
    "Overshoot/insufficient mean reversion: after a run-up into the low-70s, true prices mean-revert sharply to mid/low-60s, while the model stays anchored near 70–73. Add a mean-reversion term that pulls forecasts toward a rolling 20–30d average, with strength increasing with horizon and with distance from the mean.",
    "Downside convexity missing: large negative moves are underpredicted (tail risk on the downside). If recent volatility is high or a drawdown starts, widen the downside adjustment (e.g., subtract an extra volatility-scaled buffer for days 10–30).",
    "Lag in turning points: the model reacts too slowly to the transition from uptrend to selloff. Trigger a faster slope flip: when short-term trend (3–5d) crosses below medium-term trend (10d), impose a negative drift on the forecast trajectory rather than flat/positive drift."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~3–8 EUR), especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: when the market transitions into a drawdown, the model keeps projecting elevated/flat prices (lags the downturn). Add a regime rule: if recent returns are negative and volatility rising, tilt the forecast slope downward earlier.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following short-term spikes/rallies in history, the model extrapolates strength; realized prices mean-revert and then sell off. Cap upside continuation and introduce mean-reversion toward the 20–30d average after sharp up-moves.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–7) errors are smaller; mid/long horizon (days 15–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-weighted correction: small adjustment in week 1, larger downward adjustment from week 2 onward.",
    "Underestimation of downside tail: realized paths exhibit deeper declines than forecast (model lacks drawdown amplitude). Add a downside convexity adjustment: widen lower bands / shift quantiles down when recent momentum is negative."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-change blind spot: when the last ~5–10 days show a rollover after a local peak, the model keeps forecasting a stable-to-rising plateau. Apply a downward regime-shift adjustment starting around forecast day 10–15 (i.e., don’t extrapolate the prior uptrend).",
    "Horizon-dependent positive bias: forecasts are close in the first week but become increasingly too high beyond ~day 10, with large overestimation by days 15–30. Use an increasing negative bias correction with horizon (small in days 1–7, moderate in days 8–14, large in days 15–30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a sharp upward move (local spike to low-70s), the model predicts continued strength/mean reversion upward, but realized prices mean-revert downward strongly. After a spike, cap upside and tilt the path downward (stronger mean reversion).",
    "Underestimation of downside volatility: the model’s forecast path is too smooth and fails to capture the magnitude of drawdowns. Increase expected drawdown amplitude (wider downside bands / steeper negative drift) when recent volatility rises.",
    "Level anchoring error: the model anchors to the recent high-60s/low-70s level and is slow to follow a transition to mid-60s. If realized prices break below recent support (e.g., prior 10–20d lows), shift the entire forecast level down rather than gradually."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: the model systematically overpredicts levels (often by ~+1 to +10 EUR) and fails to price sustained drawdowns; apply a downward level shift, increasing with horizon.",
    "Regime-change blindness: after a local peak/spike (e.g., ~72–73), the model extrapolates strength, but realized prices mean-revert and then trend lower; introduce a post-spike mean-reversion penalty (stronger when the last 5–10 days are up).",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors grow materially after ~day 10–15 as the model keeps forecasts near 71–74 while outcomes fall into mid-60s; use a horizon ramp-down (progressively larger negative adjustment beyond 2 weeks).",
    "Trend-lag in downturns: when recent history starts rolling over, the model reacts too slowly and maintains elevated paths; add a momentum filter that reduces forecasts when 5–10d momentum turns negative.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: the model underestimates downside tail risk and overestimates persistence of rallies; cap upside persistence and allow faster downside drift (higher negative beta than positive beta to recent returns)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10; apply a downward level shift (increasing with horizon).",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: model extrapolates recent strength and fails to capture subsequent sell-off; add a regime check—if recent prices are elevated after an upswing, enforce mean-reversion rather than continuation.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following local highs (e.g., 71–73), forecasts stay near/above the peak while outcomes revert sharply; cap upside and increase downside adjustment after a spike.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small, but medium/long horizon errors become large and one-sided; use a correction that ramps down forecasts with lead time (e.g., linear or piecewise by week).",
    "Volatility asymmetry: model underestimates downside volatility and drawdown speed; widen lower confidence band / increase negative shock sensitivity when recent returns turn negative."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high vs. realized prices (often by ~5–12 EUR), especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift to the full path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miss: when recent history is flat-to-down, the model still projects sideways-to-up. Add a mean-reversion term that pulls forecasts toward (or below) the recent 10–20d average when the last 2–3 weeks show a downtrend.",
    "Turning-point lag: after the market rolls over, the model keeps forecasting elevated levels for ~1–2 weeks. Detect negative momentum (e.g., 5d MA < 20d MA and falling) and accelerate the decline in the first 10–15 forecast days.",
    "Overshoot after local highs/spikes: if the last observed price is a local high (spike), the model extrapolates it. Cap near-term upside and impose a partial retracement (e.g., revert 30–60% of the last 5–10d upswing over the next 5–10 days).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors widen with lead time; use a ramped correction (small adjustment days 1–5, moderate days 6–15, largest days 16–30) rather than a constant offset.",
    "Volatility overstatement at the top: forecast paths stay high and relatively smooth while realized prices fall sharply. When downside regime is detected, increase downside slope and allow larger negative moves (fatter left tail) rather than maintaining a high plateau."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward; apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Underreaction to upside regime shifts: when history shows a trough then stabilization (low 60s) and outcomes start trending up, the model lags and misses the rebound; add a positive trend term when last 5–10 days show higher lows / improving momentum.",
    "Missed late-horizon rallies: the largest errors cluster in the final third of the 30d window where true outcomes accelerate to the high-60s; increase forecast slope after day ~15 when momentum is positive rather than mean-reverting to low 60s.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after dips: following sharp declines in history, the model keeps prices anchored near ~61–65 while the market reverts higher; reduce mean-reversion strength when volatility has recently spiked and the last observed price is near a local minimum.",
    "Asymmetric error: downside is captured reasonably in the first few days, but upside is consistently underpredicted; use asymmetric correction (larger upward adjustment than downward) conditioned on positive momentum."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +2 EUR) that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market turns up, the model follows but with insufficient slope (it misses multi-day rallies). Add a momentum term: if last 5–10 days show positive drift, increase the next 2–4 week path proportionally.",
    "Lag after regime change: following a local bottom, the model takes several days to catch up. If the last observed price is above the 10–20d average (breakout), lift the whole forecast curve, not just the first few days.",
    "Right-tail miss at long horizons: large upside moves (late-horizon spikes to ~68–70) are not captured; the model caps too low. Add an upside convexity adjustment for days 20–30 when recent volatility is elevated.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: keep days 1–7 close to model (small bias), apply moderate uplift days 8–15, and strongest uplift days 16–30 (where underprediction is largest)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last ~5–10 days show a persistent rise, the model tends to stay too low, especially from ~day 15 onward. Apply an upward drift correction that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Late-horizon level bias: the model’s day 20–30 forecasts cluster in the mid-60s while realized prices frequently re-rate into the high-60s. Add a positive level shift for horizons >~20 days when recent spot is recovering from a drawdown.",
    "Momentum/lag effect: turning points are captured with delay (model reacts slowly to regime change from downtrend to rebound). If the last 3–5 observations reverse direction vs the prior 10–15 day trend, tilt forecasts in the new direction (reduce mean-reversion strength).",
    "Spike/repricing under-capture: after a breakout (realized jump to ~68–70), the model often predicts only modest increases and misses follow-through. If price breaks above the recent 10-day high, increase the projected path by an extra step-up (stronger continuation) rather than flattening.",
    "Asymmetric error: downside is modeled better than upside in this sample (early horizons are close; errors grow positive later). Use asymmetric adjustment: larger upward corrections in bullish regimes, minimal downward correction in bearish/flat regimes."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the market transitions from a dip to a sustained rally, the model responds too slowly (lag). Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 days’ slope; if slope is positive, boost forecasts, with the boost growing with horizon.",
    "Late-horizon upside miss: days ~20–30 show the largest errors during rallies (model stays in mid-60s while outcomes move to high-60s/near-70). Use a horizon-dependent uplift (e.g., +2 to +5 EUR by day 30 in rising regimes).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: the model pulls prices back toward the mid-60s even when the true path remains elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent volatility is high and the 10–15 day trend is positive.",
    "Regime switch heuristic: if price has rebounded >3 EUR from a 10-day low and is making higher highs, treat as ‘uptrend regime’ and cap downward adjustments; otherwise keep baseline behavior."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last ~7–10 days show a clear upward drift (higher highs / higher closes), the model is systematically too low, especially from ~day 15 onward. Apply a positive drift add-on that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small/mixed, but mid-to-long horizon (days ~10–30) is persistently negative (forecast < realized). Use a ramped bias correction (e.g., +0 to +X EUR by day 30).",
    "Lag/slow reaction to regime change: after a turning point from decline to rally, the forecast path stays anchored to the prior lower regime and catches up too slowly. Heuristic: if price has rebounded >~3–4 EUR from the 10-day low, shift the whole forward curve upward immediately (level shift).",
    "Underestimation of rally amplitude after dips: following a local trough, realized prices accelerate upward more than forecast (model mean-reverts too aggressively). Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent momentum is positive (e.g., if 5-day return > 0, dampen pullback assumptions).",
    "Late-horizon ceiling effect: forecasts tend to cap in the mid-60s while realized moves into high-60s/near-70. If recent 20–30d range is widening upward, allow higher terminal levels (raise terminal anchor / reduce implicit cap)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/rally, the model stays anchored to the prior dip and underpredicts levels, with the negative bias growing with horizon (largest errors in days ~15–30). Apply an upward level shift that increases with forecast step during rising regimes.",
    "Lag in trend changes: the model reacts 3–7 days late to turning points (especially dip-to-rally). Heuristic: if short-term momentum (e.g., 5d return) is positive while 20–30d momentum is negative/flat, tilt forecasts upward immediately rather than waiting for mean reversion.",
    "Excess mean-reversion / ceiling effect: forecasts repeatedly revert toward ~63–66 even when realized prices break out to ~68–70. Rule: when price closes above recent 20d mean + ~1–1.5 std (breakout), reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small/mixed, but mid/long horizon systematically too low in these examples. Apply a horizon ramp correction (e.g., add 0 at day 1 rising to +X by day 30) conditional on positive momentum/volatility expansion.",
    "Underestimation after upside shocks: following sharp upside moves, the model does not propagate the shock forward (forecasts flatten). Rule: after a +2–3% daily move or multi-day surge, carry forward a fraction of the shock into the next 10–20 days (trend persistence) instead of reverting immediately."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small +0.2–0.5 for days 1–5; larger +1–3 for days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the last 30d history shows a rebound/upswing (as in these samples), the model lags and fails to capture the subsequent rally. Add a momentum term based on recent slope (e.g., last 5–10d change) that lifts the whole path.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: the farther out, the more the model mean-reverts back toward mid-60s while the market trends to high-60s/low-70s. Reduce mean-reversion strength (or raise the long-run anchor) when recent prices are rising.",
    "Upside spikes are underpredicted more than downside moves: realized series exhibits sharper upside extensions (peaks ~69–70) than forecast. Add an asymmetric correction: allow larger positive deviations when recent volatility is elevated and trend is positive.",
    "Path shape too flat: forecasts show muted day-to-day variability versus outcomes. Inflate forecast volatility slightly (wider swings) when recent realized volatility over the last ~10 days is high."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward; apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market transitions into an upswing, the model follows but with insufficient slope; add a positive trend/acceleration term when the last 5–10 days show rising momentum.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small, but mid/long horizon (days 10–30) underprediction grows materially; use a ramping correction (e.g., +0 to +X €/t increasing with lead time).",
    "Breakout lag: after a local bottom, the model assumes mean reversion too early and misses sustained rallies; reduce mean-reversion strength when recent lows are followed by consecutive higher closes.",
    "Upper-tail miss: the model rarely reaches the realized highs during strong rallies (caps peaks); apply a nonlinear uplift when forecast path remains below recent resistance while realized volatility is rising."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model systematically underpredicts the level, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift; bias grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, large by days ~10–30).",
    "Trend/lag error: model reacts too slowly to bullish breakouts; it forecasts flat-to-down while realized prices trend up. Add a momentum/trend-following component when the last 5–10 days slope is positive.",
    "Horizon-dependent uplift: increase forecasts progressively with lead time (e.g., minimal adjustment near-term, larger uplift mid/long-term) to correct compounding underestimation.",
    "Volatility compression: model underestimates upside amplitude during rallies (peaks are too low). Inflate forecast deviations from the recent mean when regime is risk-on/uptrend.",
    "Mean-reversion misapplied: model assumes reversion toward low 60s even when market transitions to a higher range (~67–70). Use regime switch: if price recovers above prior resistance (mid-60s) and holds, anchor mean-reversion to the new higher mean rather than the old one."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most of the 30-day horizon, with the underestimation growing as the realized uptrend strengthens.",
    "Trend/level lag: the model behaves mean-reverting around the recent history level while the true series trends upward; it fails to follow regime shifts quickly (needs a positive drift term when recent returns turn positive).",
